module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ConormalModuleEtaleRestriction
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# The map of defining ideal sheaves of a fibre product square of closed subspaces

Let
```
Z --i--→ X
|        |
f        g
↓        ↓
Z' -i'-→ X'
```
be a commutative square of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`,
in which `Z` is a closed subspace of `X` and `Z'` is a closed subspace of `X'`.  Stacks 04CP
attaches to it the canonical map `g^*𝓘' ⟶ 𝓘` of defining ideal sheaves
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafMap`) and the canonical map `f^*𝒞_{Z'/X'} ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X}` of
conormal modules (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalMap`).  Stacks 04CQ asserts that when the
square is a fibre product the map of conormal modules is surjective, and that it is an
isomorphism when `g` is flat.

This file proves the **affine-chart content of the surjectivity clause**: for the fibre product
square — the one in which `Z` is the inverse image `g⁻¹(Z')` and `f` the projection — the
canonical map of defining ideal sheaves is surjective on the sections over every commutative
diagram of étale scheme charts whose two charts are affine.  The clause of Stacks 04CQ itself is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.epi_conormalMap` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ConormalModuleBaseChangeEpi.lean`, which globalizes the
statement below over the small étale site; no `@[stacks]` attribute is claimed here.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preimageSubspace`: the inverse image `g⁻¹(Z')` of a closed subspace
  along a morphism of algebraic spaces, as a closed subspace of the source — the fibre product
  `Z' ×_{X'} X` in the shape the development's statements about closed subspaces consume.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.preimageProjection`: the projection `g⁻¹(Z') ⟶ Z'`, which is the
  vertical morphism `f` of the fibre product square.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_range_idealSheafMap`: **for any commutative square**, the
  canonical map `g^*𝓘' ⟶ 𝓘` of Stacks 04CP hits, over every chart diagram, the pullback of
  regular functions of every section of `𝓘'`.  This is the statement that the abstractly
  defined morphism out of the inverse image realizes the chartwise map `g^♯` the source
  describes, and it is what makes the surjectivity argument possible at all.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map_chartDiagram`: over a chart diagram
  with both charts affine, the defining ideal of `g⁻¹(Z')` is generated by the image of the
  defining ideal of `Z'` under pullback of regular functions along the vertical morphism.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.surjective_idealSheafMap_val_app`: consequently the canonical map
  `g^*𝓘' ⟶ 𝓘` of the fibre product square is **surjective on sections** over every chart
  diagram with both charts affine.

## Implementation notes

**The fibre product is taken as an inverse image.**  For a closed subspace `Z' ⊆ X'` and a
morphism `g : X ⟶ X'` the fibre product `Z' ×_{X'} X` is the inverse image `g⁻¹(Z')`, a closed
subspace of `X` by Stacks 083Q (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isClosedSubspace_preimage`).  So "the
square is a fibre product" is expressed below by *being* that configuration, with the square
supplied by `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalEtaleRestrictionSquare` — which, despite its name
and its use for an étale morphism one file over, assumes nothing about `g`.  This is the same
convention as `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalModule`: closed subspaces rather than morphisms
with a closed-immersion hypothesis, because that is the shape in which every statement
about the defining ideal sheaf of Stacks 03MB is available.

**Why the first result needs the two transposes undone by hand.**
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafMap` is defined as the transpose, along the Stacks 03D7
adjunction `g^* ⊣ g_*` and then along the chartwise universal property of the direct image of
Stacks 03LU, of the chartwise map `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafFMap`.  Its only available
description, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafMap_transpose`, is therefore a statement about
its double transpose, and says nothing directly about the values of
`idealSheafMap.val.app`.  The witness exhibited below is produced from that description by
running the two transposes backwards: the transpose along the adjunction is the unit followed by
the direct image of the map (`CategoryTheory.Adjunction.homEquiv_unit`), and the transpose along
the universal property is composition with the counit
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ModulePushforwardPresentation.homEquiv_apply`), whose naturality in
the source module (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ModulePushforwardPresentation.naturality`) moves the
morphism out past the counit.  The witness is the counit applied to the unit of the section.

**Affineness of both charts is needed, and only there.**  The chartwise generation statement is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_preimage_eq_map` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/LocallyPrincipalPullback.lean`, which is stated for affine
charts upstairs and downstairs: the defining ideal at a chart is the kernel on *global*
sections, and over a non-affine chart that kernel is not generated by the pullback of the one
downstairs.  Everything else in this file is chart-independent.

## What is not proved

* **The surjectivity clause of Stacks 04CQ is not proved *here*.**  Three steps separate
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.surjective_idealSheafMap_val_app` from
  `Epi (AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalMap …)`, and all three are carried out in
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ConormalModuleBaseChangeEpi.lean`.
  (1) *The covering.*  Surjectivity on sections over affine chart diagrams gives local
  surjectivity only once the source charts of those diagrams are known to generate covering
  sieves of the small étale site of `X`: one needs that the étale charts of `X` which are affine
  and admit a morphism to an *affine* étale chart of `X'` over `g` are cover-dense.  That is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.mem_topology_of_hasAffineChartDiagram` of
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/AffineChartDiagramCover.lean`.  (2) *Local surjectivity
  to epi* is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.EpiMono.epi_of_isLocallySurjective`.  (3) *The passage from
  the ideal sheaf map to the conormal map*: `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalMap` is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalPullbackComparison`, a component of a natural isomorphism,
  followed by the inverse image along the inclusion of `Z` of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.idealSheafMap`, so an epimorphism of ideal sheaves gives an
  epimorphism of conormal modules because the inverse image is a left adjoint.  The equation
  `conormalMap = conormalPullbackComparison ≫ (modulePullback i).map idealSheafMap`, a single
  delta reduction, has to be stated with the target of its second factor ascribed as
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalModule`: crossing between that spelling and the written-out
  inverse image of the defining ideal sheaf *inside* the composition exhausts the default
  heartbeat budget, while crossing it outside costs nothing.  The statement in that shape is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.conormalMap_eq`.
* **The flat clause of Stacks 04CQ is not proved**, and nothing here bears on it.  It needs the
  injectivity of `g^*𝓘' ⟶ g^*𝒪_{X'} = 𝒪_X` for flat `g`, that is the exactness of the inverse
  image of modules along a flat morphism of algebraic spaces; no statement in this development
  establishes it, and the scheme-level clause of Stacks 0473 that the source reduces it to is
  not formalized either (`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Conormal/SquareEpi.lean` carries only
  `@[stacks 0473 "surjectivity"]`).
* **Stacks 06BD is not proved in the middle.**  Its right-hand exactness is the instance of the
  surjectivity clause of Stacks 04CQ at the fibre product square of a pair of closed subspaces,
  and is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.epi_conormalMap_closedSubspaces` of
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ConormalModuleBaseChangeEpi.lean`.  Its exactness in the
  middle is not formalized at the scheme level either
  (`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Conormal/ConormalSequence.lean` carries only
  `@[stacks 062S "exactness at the right term: the canonical map 𝒞_{Z/X} ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/Y} is epi"]`).

